"""Preparation-only reference for the FlagGems ``tanh_backward`` operator.

This file is the ``--reference-source`` / ``--candidate`` injected with the
official runtime override option::

    --override tanh_backward:<this file>:run

so ``run`` temporarily replaces the public ``flag_gems.tanh_backward`` export.
It is a *reference* used to validate the pytest chain, not an optimised
candidate: it never imports ``flag_gems`` and never calls the operator under
test, and it performs no dtype cast and no CPU round trip (device and dtype of
the inputs are preserved, no input is mutated).

The frozen kunlunxin operator ABI is ``tanh_backward(grad_output, output)``
(``src/flag_gems/runtime/backend/_kunlunxin/ops/tanh.py:215``) and
``in_grad = grad_output * (1 - output * output)``.

Two frozen call conventions have to be served by this single entrypoint, and
both of them are declared by the frozen suites (no ABI is invented here):

1. Accuracy — master ``tests/test_tanh.py::test_tanh_backward`` calls the
   public export directly::

       ref_in_grad = torch.ops.aten.tanh_backward(ref_grad, ref_out)
       res_in_grad = flag_gems.tanh_backward(res_grad, res_out)

   so the two-argument arm returns exactly ``torch.ops.aten.tanh_backward``,
   the same oracle the frozen test itself declares.

2. Core benchmark — ``benchmark/test_tanh.py::test_tanh_backward`` builds
   ``base.UnaryPointwiseBenchmark(op_name="tanh_backward",
   torch_op=torch.tanh, is_backward=True)``. That harness yields a single
   input tensor (``benchmark/base.py:1513-1516``), calls the timed callable as
   ``op(x)`` and then differentiates it through ``torch.autograd.grad``
   (``benchmark/base.py:347-362``). The one-tensor callable declared by that
   frozen benchmark is ``torch.tanh`` itself (its ``torch_op``, which is also
   the callable the package's own ``--ref-as-candidate`` mode selects for this
   benchmark), so the unary arm returns ``torch.tanh(x)``. The harness then
   times the tanh-backward kernel through autograd, exactly as it does for the
   frozen native baseline.

   Note: the frozen benchmark cannot express the two-argument operator ABI —
   with a real ``tanh_backward`` candidate it calls ``op(x)`` and raises
   ``TypeError``. Adapting the reference to the harness' declared unary
   convention is a preparation-side ABI adapter, recorded as such in
   ``review-manifest.json``; the frozen ``benchmark/test_tanh.py`` and
   ``benchmark/base.py`` are left unchanged.
"""

import torch

_SENTINEL = object()


def run(grad_output, output=_SENTINEL):
    """Reference ``tanh_backward(grad_output, output)`` plus the harness' unary arm."""
    if output is not _SENTINEL:
        # Accuracy ABI: tanh_backward(grad_output, output) -> in_grad.
        return torch.ops.aten.tanh_backward(grad_output, output)
    # Benchmark ABI: the frozen harness' unary callable (``torch_op=torch.tanh``,
    # ``is_backward=True``), timed through autograd.
    return torch.tanh(grad_output)
